title: Counting to three: your first state machine
mode: draw
chapter: Counting
check: behaves
parts: dff and not
---- intro
Not every counter goes round at a power of two. This one counts **0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2** ... and never shows 3.

When you cannot just flip bits, write down where each state goes next, and build each next bit from the table:

| now q1 | now q0 | next q1 | next q0 |
|--------|--------|---------|---------|
| 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 |
| 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 |

Read the columns: next `q1` is just `q0`. Next `q0` is 1 only when both are 0. A circuit that steps through states from a table like this is a **state machine**.
---- task
Make `q1 q0` count 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2 ... one step per tick.
---- start
clock clk 0 200
led q0 520 40
led q1 520 180
---- reference
clock clk
led q0, q1
wire a[1], b[1]
a = dff(~a & ~b, clk)
b = dff(a, clk)
q0 = a
q1 = b
---- answer
clock clk 0 200
not a0 120 0
not a1 120 90
and g0 240 40
dff f0 360 40
dff f1 360 180
led q0 520 40
led q1 520 180
f0.q -> a0.in
f1.q -> a1.in
a0 -> g0.0
a1 -> g0.1
g0 -> f0.d
clk -> f0.clk
f0.q -> q0
f0.q -> f1.d
clk -> f1.clk
f1.q -> q1
---- hint1
The second flip-flop's `d` is the first flip-flop's `q`: next `q1` is `q0`.
---- hint2
The first flip-flop's `d` is NOT `q0` AND NOT `q1`: two NOT gates and an AND.
---- solution
Next `q1` = `q0`, so the first flip-flop feeds the second. Next `q0` = NOT `q0` AND NOT `q1`, so it is 1 only after state 0. Step: 0, 1, 2, 0.
---- done
You designed a state machine from a table: write each state and the one after it, then build each next bit from its column. Traffic lights, vending machines and the control unit of a processor are all made this way. From a switch and a light to here, all of it drawn.
